<section class="guide" aria-label="3D export guide"><div class="eyebrow">BLENDER → SUBSTANCE PAINTER → WEB</div><h2 style="margin-top:12px">Make it detailed. Keep it light.</h2><p>Practical starting budgets for one featured model on mobile. These are studio targets, not universal limits: texture memory, materials, screen resolution and the device matter too.</p><div class="budget-grid"><div><strong>20–50k</strong><span>triangles per model</span></div><div><strong>1K–2K</strong><span>texture resolution</span></div><div><strong>2–5 MB</strong><span>complete GLB target</span></div></div><p class="small">Simple props: aim for 5–20k triangles. Detailed hero pieces: 50–100k only after phone testing. Start with 1–4 materials and fewer than 10 draw calls. The panel warns above 100k triangles or 5 MB; 15 MB is the upload ceiling.</p><details open><summary>Blender: prepare the web mesh</summary><ol><li>Keep a separate high-poly source. Retopologize or carefully decimate the web copy, preserving its silhouette. Bake fine detail into normal maps instead of exporting dense geometry.</li><li>Count <strong>triangles</strong>, not quads. Check the evaluated mesh after modifiers. A quad normally becomes two triangles; exported vertices may split at UV seams and hard edges.</li><li>Apply scale on the export copy, check orientation and normals, remove hidden geometry, and unwrap UVs. Export only the intended objects, without unnecessary cameras or lights.</li><li>Use Principled BSDF with image textures. Procedural materials need baking. Export <strong>glTF 2.0 → GLB</strong> to package the mesh and textures in one file.</li></ol></details><details><summary>Substance Painter: bake and export textures</summary><ol><li>Bake high-poly details onto the low-poly mesh. Start at 1024 px; use 2048 px when a close-up benefits. Avoid 4K/8K textures for the default mobile model.</li><li>Use a PBR metallic/roughness workflow with OpenGL normal maps (+Y). Match the exported textures to Blender’s Principled material before GLB export.</li><li>Base color and emissive use sRGB. Normal, roughness, metallic and AO are data maps; use Non-Color in Blender. For packed ORM: red = occlusion, green = roughness, blue = metallic.</li><li>Prefer opaque materials. Alpha blending, transmission, many material slots and multiple texture sets can cost more than extra triangles. A single uncompressed 2K RGBA texture uses roughly 16 MiB before mipmaps.</li></ol></details><details><summary>Compress, preview and verify</summary><ol><li>Use Draco or Meshopt for geometry when the saved transfer size justifies decoding. This viewer supports both, plus KTX2/Basis texture compression. Keep the original source and verify compressed output.</li><li>Create a WebP cover around 100–250 KB. Visitors see the cover first; the viewer code and model only download after they open the 3D preview.</li><li>Test the textured GLB on a real midrange phone and a throttled connection. Check loading time, responsiveness, memory and material appearance; reduce textures or materials if interaction stutters.</li><li>Aim for smooth 30 FPS or better while orbiting on your target phone. The viewer caps pixel density at 1.5, renders on demand, and stops optional rotation when hidden.</li></ol><p class="small">References: <a href="https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/addons/import_export/scene_gltf2.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Blender glTF export</a> · <a href="https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-painter/using/export/output-templates/default-output-templates/default-presets" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Substance Painter export presets</a> · <a href="https://threejs.org/docs/#GLTFLoader" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Three.js GLTFLoader</a></p></details></section>